import json, re
T = json.loads(r'''{"Q2": "Table 7 lists the ten most abundant species of the Kraken2/Bracken profile. The MetaPhlAn profile was produced on Galaxy and is provided in the repository (03_results/sample_metaphlan.txt); this report does not interpret its individual values. The expected differences follow from the principle of each tool. Kraken2 assigns each read to the lowest common ancestor of the reference genomes that share its k-mers (Kraken 2 stores minimizers of these k-mers in a compact hash table), and Bracken redistributes the reads assigned to higher ranks to species level. The output is therefore an estimate of the proportion of reads, computed against a RefSeq-based database (Standard-8 here) and including the unclassified reads (47.31%) in the total. MetaPhlAn maps reads to clade-specific marker genes (about 5.1 million markers for 26,970 species-level genome bins in MetaPhlAn 4), computes the coverage of each clade as a robust average over its markers and normalises it across the detected clades; the handout describes the result as a proportion of cells rather than a proportion of reads. MetaPhlAn also discards reads shorter than 70 bp by default, which affects part of this dataset (reads of 50 to 143 bp). The two databases and their taxonomies differ (RefSeq names versus SGB names), so the same organism can carry different names, and a taxon absent from one database appears only in the other tool. Finally, the MetaPhlAn 4 benchmark reports that Bracken tends to give many false positives unless low-abundance hits are filtered, so the low-abundance species of the Bracken list should be interpreted with caution.", "Q3": "Kraken2 left 42,561 of 89,953 read pairs unclassified (47.31%) and classified 47,392 pairs (52.69%). Possible explanations, not tested individually: the Standard-8 database is capped at 8 GB and contains less reference information than the full Standard database; many gut organisms are uncultured or missing from RefSeq; the reads are short and of variable length (50 to 143 bp) and Kraken2 needs exact k-mer matches, so sequencing errors reduce the number of hits; and organisms outside the database (fungi, phages, plasmids) cannot be assigned.", "Q4": "The HUMAnN functional profile was not obtained, so the five most abundant MetaCyc pathways are not reported and no pathway names are given. HUMAnN 3.9 was installed locally (it required a Python 3.12 environment), but the databases (ChocoPhlAn, UniRef90) are very large and the protein search needs more memory than the machine had (about 2.8 GB of RAM under WSL2). On Galaxy, HUMAnN stopped with database and profile version errors that I could not resolve (see section 5 of the PDF report). The planned command is documented in scripts/run_tp2.sh.", "Q5": "The read-based approach only detects what is already present in the reference databases. In a poorly described environmental microbiome most organisms are uncultured, so many reads cannot be assigned (even in this well-studied gut sample, 47% of the read pairs were unclassified with Standard-8). Reads of novel organisms can also be assigned to the closest known relative, which biases the profile. Short reads give little gene context and cannot reconstruct genomes, so new genes and pathways are missed, and functional profiling is limited to genes already annotated in ChocoPhlAn or UniRef. Assembly-based approaches (TP 3) can discover new organisms, at the cost of heavier computation and the need for sufficient coverage.", "AI": "## 6. AI question (TP 2)\n\n### 6.1 Assistant and prompt\n\nAssistant: Claude (Anthropic), used in the same chat session that was used to prepare this report. The prompt was written from the instruction of the TP and the results of this analysis.\n\nPrompt: I analysed one human gut shotgun metagenome (89,953 read pairs, Illumina, reads of 50 to 143 bp after cleaning) with Kraken2 (Standard-8 database) and Bracken, and with MetaPhlAn (run on Galaxy). Kraken2 left 47.31% of the read pairs unclassified. Top Bracken species: Agathobacter rectalis 25.03%, Faecalibacterium prausnitzii 11.34%, Roseburia intestinalis 7.65%, Bifidobacterium longum 5.46%, Phocaeicola vulgatus 4.35%. Explain the differences to expect between the two profiles: reference database, unit of measure (proportion of reads or relative abundance of genomes) and taxa absent from the database. Do the two tools measure the same thing?\n\n### 6.2 Extract of the AI response\n\nKraken2 assigns each read to the lowest common ancestor of the reference genomes that share its k-mers (k = 35 by default), and Bracken then redistributes reads to species level, so the result is a proportion of reads. Its reference is whatever database you build or download (here Standard-8, RefSeq-based, capped at 8 GB), so organisms absent from it stay unclassified (47.31% here). MetaPhlAn aligns reads to about 5 million clade-specific marker genes and estimates abundance from marker coverage normalised by marker length, so the result is closer to a proportion of cells than of reads. MetaPhlAn only reports the fraction of the community that it can classify, so its percentages sum to 100% over detected taxa, whereas Kraken2 percentages include the unclassified reads. Organisms with large genomes are likely to be over-represented in the Kraken2/Bracken profile relative to MetaPhlAn, and taxa absent from the MetaPhlAn marker database will be missing from its profile even if they are present in RefSeq.\n\n### 6.3 Verification with primary sources\n\n- Wood et al., 2019, Genome Biology 20:257, doi:10.1186/s13059-019-1891-0. Kraken 2 maps minimizers (substrings of length l, with l up to k) to lowest common ancestors in a probabilistic compact hash table, whereas Kraken 1 stored all k-mers. The paper also reports the memory cost: for a reference of 9.1 Gbp, 10.6 GB of memory for Kraken 2 classification against 72.4 GB for Kraken 1. This confirms the lowest-common-ancestor principle and refines the AI statement about k-mers.\n- Blanco-Miguez et al., 2023, Nature Biotechnology 41:1633-1644, doi:10.1038/s41587-023-01688-w. Results: 5.1 million unique marker genes spanning 26,970 species-level genome bins (SGBs), from a catalog of 1.01 million genomes and MAGs, with an average of 189 markers per SGB. Methods: reads are mapped with Bowtie 2; reads shorter than 70 bp and alignments with a MAPQ below 5 are discarded by default; clade coverage is a robust average of marker coverage that excludes the top and bottom 20% of markers; coverages are normalised across the detected clades to give relative abundances; an optional feature estimates the fraction of unclassified reads. Discussion: reference-based methods only detect species present in the catalog, and viruses and eukaryotes are not extensively included.\n- TP 2 handout (Kraken2 and MetaPhlAn sections): k = 35 for Kraken2, and MetaPhlAn abundance described as a proportion of cells rather than a proportion of reads.\n\n### 6.4 Errors and approximations found in the AI response\n\n- \"k-mers\": Kraken 2 compares minimizers derived from k-mers (Wood et al.). Approximation.\n- \"Normalised by marker length\": the paper describes a robust average of marker coverage normalised across clades; no separate normalisation by marker length is described. Approximation.\n- \"Proportion of cells\": the Blanco-Miguez paper presents relative abundance derived from normalised coverage and does not use the term cells; this wording comes from the handout. Not confirmed by the primary source.\n- \"MetaPhlAn only reports the classified fraction\": true for the default relative abundances, but incomplete, because MetaPhlAn 4 has an optional estimate of the unclassified read fraction. Approximation.\n- \"Large genomes over-represented in Kraken2/Bracken\": plausible reasoning but supported by neither paper. Unverified inference.\n- Omission: MetaPhlAn discards reads shorter than 70 bp by default, which is relevant here (reads of 50 to 143 bp).\n- Omission: the MetaPhlAn 4 benchmark reports many false positives for Bracken unless hits below 0.01% relative abundance are filtered, which matters when reading the Bracken species list.\n- The description of Standard-8 (RefSeq-based, 8 GB cap) cannot be checked in the two papers.\n\n### 6.5 Does the AI confuse read abundance with cellular abundance?\n\nNo. The AI separates read abundance from cellular abundance correctly in principle, and it does not mix the two units. However, the justification it gives (normalisation by marker length, proportion of cells) is only partly supported by the primary source, which describes relative abundance derived from normalised marker coverage.\n\n### 6.6 Conclusion: the most suitable tool\n\nFor the question asked in this TP (which species are present in a human gut sample and in what proportions), MetaPhlAn 4 is the better-suited tool for species-level relative abundance: its marker catalogue is designed for this purpose and its authors report better accuracy than Bracken on synthetic communities (an evaluation done by the tool's own authors). Kraken2/Bracken is faster and more flexible, gives an explicit unclassified fraction, and can use any database, but its results depend on database content and size (here only 8 GB) and need filtering of low-abundance hits. The two tools measure different quantities, so their percentages should not be compared directly.\n", "REFS": "- Wood DE, Lu J, Langmead B. Improved metagenomic analysis with Kraken 2. Genome Biol. 2019;20:257. doi:10.1186/s13059-019-1891-0\n- Lu J, Breitwieser FP, Thielen P, Salzberg SL. Bracken: estimating species abundance in metagenomics data. PeerJ Comput Sci. 2017;3:e104. doi:10.7717/peerj-cs.104\n- Blanco-Miguez A, et al. Extending and improving metagenomic taxonomic profiling with uncharacterized species using MetaPhlAn 4. Nat Biotechnol. 2023;41:1633-1644. doi:10.1038/s41587-023-01688-w\n- Beghini F, et al. Integrating taxonomic, functional, and strain-level profiling of diverse microbial communities with bioBakery 3. eLife. 2021;10:e65088. doi:10.7554/eLife.65088\n- Chen S, Zhou Y, Chen Y, Gu J. fastp: an ultra-fast all-in-one FASTQ preprocessor. Bioinformatics. 2018;34:i884-i890. doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/bty560\n- Langmead B, Salzberg SL. Fast gapped-read alignment with Bowtie 2. Nat Methods. 2012;9:357-359. doi:10.1038/nmeth.1923\n- Ewels P, Magnusson M, Lundin S, Kaller M. MultiQC: summarize analysis results for multiple tools and samples in a single report. Bioinformatics. 2016;32:3047-3048. doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btw354\n- Ondov BD, Bergman NH, Phillippy AM. Interactive metagenomic visualization in a Web browser. BMC Bioinformatics. 2011;12:385. doi:10.1186/1471-2105-12-385\n- The Galaxy Community. The Galaxy platform for accessible, reproducible, and collaborative data analyses: 2024 update. Nucleic Acids Res. 2024;52:W83-W94. doi:10.1093/nar/gkae410", "MODALITY": "**Modality:** Command line (conda, WSL2 Ubuntu, VS Code) for QC, cleaning, host removal and Kraken2/Bracken; Galaxy (usegalaxy.eu) for MetaPhlAn and HUMAnN because of memory limits (about 2.8 GB RAM)"}''')
s = open("report.md", encoding="utf-8").read()

for n, key in ((2, "Q2"), (3, "Q3"), (4, "Q4"), (5, "Q5")):
    s = re.sub(r"(\*\*Question %d\.\*\*.*?\*\*Answer:\*\*\n)TODO[^\n]*" % n,
               lambda m, k=key: m.group(1) + T[k], s, count=1, flags=re.S)

s = re.sub(r"## 6\. AI question \(TP 2\).*?(?=\n## References)", lambda m: T["AI"], s, flags=re.S)
s = re.sub(r"(## References\n)- TODO[^\n]*", lambda m: m.group(1) + T["REFS"], s)
